from ./Components import Components
from ./Compositions import Compositions
from ./Errors import Errors
from ./HtmlOutput import HtmlOutput
from ./Values import Values

export anchor Conditions:
    description:
        Conditions let one component look different depending on an attribute
        set on its instance. A component declares the attributes it accepts,
        and `when` blocks inside it override properties for some values.

    purpose:
        A design often needs a few versions of one component, such as a small
        and a large button. With `extends`, each version is a separate
        component. With conditions, the versions live in one component, and
        the instance picks one by setting an attribute, as in
        `\ Button { size: small } \`.

        Both ways are allowed. Use `extends` when a version is its own thing
        with its own name. Use a condition when the versions are settings of
        the same thing.

    declaration:
        A component declares an attribute on its own line, written as the
        word `attribute`, the attribute's name, a colon, the allowed values
        separated by `|`, an `=`, and the default value:
        `attribute size: small | medium | large = medium`.

        - name
            Follows the rules for attribute names in @{Compositions}:
            lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter. It
            can't be the name of a component property, or `class`, `style`
            or `src`.
        - values
            Each value is lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting
            with a letter. A list needs at least two values, and no value
            may appear twice.
        - default
            Required. It must be one of the allowed values. It is used when
            an instance does not set the attribute.

        A component may declare any number of attributes, each with a
        different name. Attribute lines may sit anywhere among the property
        lines, since the order of lines has no meaning.

    whenBlocks:
        A `when` block is the word `when`, a condition, and a colon. The lines
        indented beneath it are property lines, written exactly as at the top
        level of the component. They apply only when the condition is true
        for the instance.

        A block holds property lines only. It can't hold attribute
        declarations, other `when` blocks, or the htmlTag property, because an
        element's tag never depends on an attribute. Within one block, a
        property may be set only once, and the shorthand rule in
        @{Components} applies as usual.

    conditions:
        A condition compares a declared attribute with one of its allowed
        values, using `==` or `!=`, as in `size == small`. Comparisons are
        combined with `&&`, `||` and `!`, and grouped with parentheses, with
        the meaning and precedence they have in @{Values}. The condition is
        not wrapped in parentheses as a whole.

        - names
            The left side of a comparison is the name of an attribute
            declared by the component or one it extends.
        - values
            The right side is one of that attribute's allowed values.
        - only attributes
            Numbers, sizes and arithmetic can't appear in a condition. A
            condition only reads attributes.

    instances:
        An instance sets a declared attribute in its body like any other
        attribute, as in `\ Button { size: small, "Save" } \`. The value must
        be one of the allowed values. It may be quoted. An instance that does
        not set the attribute gets the default.

    resolution:
        An instance's style is worked out in this order:

        - base
            The component's plain properties, merged along the whole extends
            chain as described in @{Components}, with the shorthand rule
            applied.
        - blocks
            Every `when` block in the chain whose condition is true for the
            instance is applied on top. A property set in a block replaces
            the base value. A shorthand in a block replaces every property it
            covers, even one set directly in the base.

        Blocks always win over base properties, wherever each came from. So a
        component that extends another and sets its own `padding` does not
        undo the `padding` of an inherited block for `small`.

    clashes:
        Two blocks may both be true for the same instance. The compiler checks
        every combination of allowed values to find these pairs.

        - same component
            If two such blocks are declared in the same component and set the
            same property, or a shorthand and a property it covers, that is
            an error. The order of the blocks never decides. Fix it by
            narrowing one condition, as in `size == small && tone == plain`.
        - along the chain
            A block in a component overrides a block in a component it
            extends. This is ordinary overriding and is not an error.
        - several bases
            When a component extends more than one component, their blocks
            are merged left to right, and the last in line wins, the same as
            for properties.

    inheritance:
        A component inherits the attributes declared by the components it
        extends, with their values and default.

        - default
            A component may change the default of an inherited attribute,
            with a line that names the attribute and a new default, as in
            `attribute size = small`.
        - values
            A component can't change the allowed values of an inherited
            attribute. Declaring it again with a values list is an error.
        - new attributes
            A component may declare new attributes of its own, and add `when`
            blocks for inherited or new attributes.
        - same name
            When two components it extends both declare an attribute with
            the same name, their values lists must be the same, or it is an
            error. The default of the last in line wins.

    output:
        Conditions are resolved before any output is written, so SVG and PNG
        output simply draw each instance with its resolved style. HTML
        output is described in @{HtmlOutput}.

    errors:
        The errors for conditions are listed in @{Errors}.

    example:
        ```emul
        \\\
        component Button:
            htmlTag: button
            attribute size: small | large = large
            attribute tone: plain | danger = plain
            padding: 8px 12px
            fontSize: 14px
            backgroundColor: #e4e4e7

            when size == small:
                padding: 4px 8px
                fontSize: 12px

            when tone == danger:
                backgroundColor: #dc2626
                color: #ffffff

        composition Toolbar {
            Row {
                Button { "Save" }
                Button { size: small, "Cancel" }
                Button { size: small, tone: danger, "Delete" }
            }
        }
        \\\
        ```

        `Save` uses the defaults, so it has the base style. `Cancel` is small.
        `Delete` is small and red. Both of its blocks are true, but they set
        different properties, so they do not clash. If the `danger` block also
        set `padding`, that would be a `conflicting-conditions` error.

    openQuestions:
        compositionAttributes:
            Should a composition instance be able to pass attributes on to
            the instances inside it? Today its body must be empty.
        requiredAttributes:
            Should an attribute with no sensible default be allowed, making
            the attribute required on every instance?
        flagAttributes:
            Should there be on and off attributes, written without a values
            list and read as `when compact` with no comparison?
